import{_ as t,o as a,c as s,ao as i}from"./chunks/framework.BWj6JZUP.js";const g=JSON.parse('{"title":"Correlating scenes larger than memory","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"howto/large-scenes.md","filePath":"howto/large-scenes.md","lastUpdated":null}'),o={name:"howto/large-scenes.md"};function r(n,e,h,l,d,c){return a(),s("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[i('<h1 id="Correlating-scenes-larger-than-memory" tabindex="-1">Correlating scenes larger than memory <a class="header-anchor" href="#Correlating-scenes-larger-than-memory" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Correlating scenes larger than memory {#Correlating-scenes-larger-than-memory}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>A pass over a whole scene allocates arrays the size of the scene. <code>process_block_size</code> correlates the scene a block at a time instead. This lowers peak memory and gives the same answer: the result is bit-identical to the untiled call. Only peak memory, the block shape, buffer reuse and the threading shape differ.</p><div class="language-julia vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">julia</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">out </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> autorift</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(image1, image2; chip_size </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> 32</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">, process_block_size </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> (</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">1024</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">, </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">1024</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">))</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Blocking can lower peak memory even when the scene would fit. For a file-backed <code>Raster</code>, <code>process_block_size</code> defaults to a size derived from the halo and the file&#39;s chunking (see <a href="/AutoRIFT.jl/dev/reference/correlating#AutoRIFT.block_size_for"><code>AutoRIFT.block_size_for</code></a>). For any other input it defaults to <code>nothing</code>, one block over the whole scene. An unblocked run throws an <code>ArgumentError</code> for an array that is still on disk (<a href="/AutoRIFT.jl/dev/reference/correlating#AutoRIFT.ondisk"><code>AutoRIFT.ondisk</code></a>), because it would read the array one pixel at a time.</p><h2 id="Choosing-a-block-size" tabindex="-1">Choosing a block size <a class="header-anchor" href="#Choosing-a-block-size" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Choosing a block size {#Choosing-a-block-size}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Peak memory is set by a block&#39;s area, not by the number of blocks. A threaded run holds nine arrays sized to the largest read window per task, and the task count is capped at <code>min(nblocks, nthreads)</code>, so the footprint is area × threads however finely the scene is cut. A run with <code>threaded = false</code> holds one such set.</p><p>Total process peak on a 17121 × 16961 Landsat overlap at 10 threads, from a memory-mapped input, against an untiled run of the same scene:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">block</th><th style="text-align:right;">blocks</th><th style="text-align:right;">peak</th><th style="text-align:right;">runtime</th><th style="text-align:right;">read amplification</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;">untiled</td><td style="text-align:right;">1</td><td style="text-align:right;">4958 MiB</td><td style="text-align:right;">20.5 s</td><td style="text-align:right;">1.00×</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">2048 px</td><td style="text-align:right;">81</td><td style="text-align:right;">3116 MiB</td><td style="text-align:right;">24.4 s</td><td style="text-align:right;">1.13×</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">1024 px</td><td style="text-align:right;">289</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>2140 MiB</strong></td><td style="text-align:right;">22.6 s</td><td style="text-align:right;">1.26×</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">512 px</td><td style="text-align:right;">1089</td><td style="text-align:right;">2248 MiB</td><td style="text-align:right;">22.3 s</td><td style="text-align:right;">1.55×</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">256 px</td><td style="text-align:right;">4160</td><td style="text-align:right;">2042 MiB</td><td style="text-align:right;">24.0 s</td><td style="text-align:right;">2.21×</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Peak stays between 2042 and 2248 MiB across a 14× range of block counts. <code>(1024, 1024)</code> is a good general choice: its peak is near the minimum, and smaller blocks gain nothing further.</p><h2 id="Runtime-is-set-by-the-halo" tabindex="-1">Runtime is set by the halo <a class="header-anchor" href="#Runtime-is-set-by-the-halo" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Runtime is set by the halo {#Runtime-is-set-by-the-halo}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A block reads beyond its own edges, because a chip near the boundary needs the search window around it. That fixed-width margin is the halo. The imagery a run reads grows as</p><div class="language- vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>((block + 2·halo) / block)²</span></span></code></pre></div><p>which is the last column above, at a 69-pixel halo. Below 1024 px the memory curve is flat while the redundant reading keeps growing. Read amplification also raises the allocation rate: a 128-pixel block on this scene spends its time in garbage collection and had not finished after 20 minutes, against 22.6 s at 1024 px.</p><p>A wide halo therefore needs a proportionally larger block. A block only a few halos across is mostly overlap, and a block smaller than its own halo throws an <code>ArgumentError</code>.</p><div class="language-julia vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">julia</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">halo</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(grid, p, </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">size</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(image1))   </span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;"># the margin this configuration needs, in pixels, per axis</span></span></code></pre></div><p><code>AutoRIFT.halo(p)</code> gives the same answer from the parameters alone, without building a grid.</p><p>The halo need not be square. A wide search radius in x and a narrow one in y give an asymmetric halo. On a NISAR L2 granule (halo 2216 × 1103), a <code>2304 × 1152</code> block follows it and beats a square block of larger area on peak, runtime and thread occupancy at once. Size the two axes separately against <code>halo</code>. The halo is the minimum, not the target.</p><h2 id="Keep-the-block-count-above-the-thread-count" tabindex="-1">Keep the block count above the thread count <a class="header-anchor" href="#Keep-the-block-count-above-the-thread-count" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Keep the block count above the thread count {#Keep-the-block-count-above-the-thread-count}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>With fewer blocks than threads, every block is in flight at once. On the scene above at 8192 px, nine 8331² working sets were held at once, for a peak of <strong>14856 MiB, 3× the untiled run</strong>. A block size that is too large uses more memory than not blocking.</p><p>Blocks are also the unit of threaded work, and their cost varies by orders of magnitude, so a small pool waits on its slowest block. Occupancy of ten threads, by blocks per thread:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">blocks/thread</th><th style="text-align:right;">10</th><th style="text-align:right;">38</th><th style="text-align:right;">76</th><th style="text-align:right;">148</th><th style="text-align:right;">230</th><th style="text-align:right;">250</th><th style="text-align:right;">581</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;">threads busy</td><td style="text-align:right;">2.3</td><td style="text-align:right;">4.6</td><td style="text-align:right;">6.6</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>8.9</strong></td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>9.0</strong></td><td style="text-align:right;">7.8</td><td style="text-align:right;">9.0</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Aim for 100–250 blocks per thread. The 581 row reaches full occupancy at 21.6× read amplification, which costs more than it gains.</p><p>A block holds a whole number of grid points, so the size is an upper bound on the block&#39;s span: a block holds as many grid points as fit within the requested pixels. Pass a tuple <code>(X, Y)</code> or <code>(X = …, Y = …)</code>, never a scalar. A full-width band pays halo on two sides where a square block pays on four, so the shape is the caller&#39;s choice.</p><p>A preprocessing filter that estimates from the whole image cannot be reproduced block by block. It is rejected with an <code>ArgumentError</code>. See <a href="/AutoRIFT.jl/dev/reference/parameters#AutoRIFT.filter_reach"><code>AutoRIFT.filter_reach</code></a>.</p><h2 id="Reading-a-pair-that-is-still-on-disk" tabindex="-1">Reading a pair that is still on disk <a class="header-anchor" href="#Reading-a-pair-that-is-still-on-disk" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Reading a pair that is still on disk {#Reading-a-pair-that-is-still-on-disk}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>For input already in memory, including a memory-mapped array, the filtered scene is never formed. For input still on disk, each block&#39;s window is read once per pass (a coarse and a fine pass at each of three chip sizes). On a 15901 × 13435 GeoTIFF pair this reads 3.4–4.4× the scene over a run, against 0.66–0.96× for a single pass. No block size removes the repeat reads.</p><p><code>cache_budget</code> decides whether to read the pair into memory once instead.</p><div class="language-julia vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">julia</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">out </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> autorift</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(r1, r2; process_block_size </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> (</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">1024</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">, </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">1024</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">), cache_budget </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> :auto</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">)</span></span></code></pre></div><p><code>:auto</code>, the default, compares two volumes that can be computed before anything is read: the pair&#39;s own bytes, and the sum of every block&#39;s read window once per pass. It caches the pair when the first is smaller. The budget is capped at an eighth of free memory, divided by the thread count when <code>threaded = false</code>, since each task then decides for itself.</p><p>Either the whole pair is cached or none of it is, so a byte count smaller than the pair caches nothing. A blocked run sweeps every block once per pass, so a chunk&#39;s next use is a whole sweep away, and a cache smaller than the whole working set has evicted it by then. Replaying the requests for the 15901 × 13435 pair at blocks of 1024 gives a read volume of 3.4× the scene at half the working set and 5.7× at a twentieth. It falls below 1× only when the whole pair is cached.</p><p>Caching a 0.398 GiB pair raised peak from <strong>3.6 to 4.2 GiB</strong> at blocks of 512 px, and cut the runtime from <strong>15.2 s to 13.5 s</strong> at 10 threads. The gain shrinks as the block grows: at 2048 px the runtime is 14.1 s either way, since a larger block reads less to begin with.</p><ul><li><p>Set a byte count when free memory is a poor guide to what this process may use, as in a container with a cgroup limit or a host shared with work Julia cannot see.</p></li><li><p>Set <code>nothing</code> to hold peak to the blocks alone.</p></li><li><p>Memory-map the input to keep the raw pair off the heap. A resident input is never copied, and the kernel can reclaim a mapped array&#39;s pages under pressure, which it cannot do for an <code>Array</code>.</p></li></ul><p>Input already in memory is never copied whatever <code>cache_budget</code> says, and the result does not depend on it.</p><h2 id="What-reading-from-disk-costs" tabindex="-1">What reading from disk costs <a class="header-anchor" href="#What-reading-from-disk-costs" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What reading from disk costs {#What-reading-from-disk-costs}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The answer is bit-identical to the same pair held in memory, at any block size.</p><p>On a Landsat 8/9 pair over Jakobshavn (17121 × 16961 <code>Float32</code>, 4.48 M grid points, 10 threads), reading from the two GeoTIFFs peaks at <strong>5.4 GiB against 9.0 GiB</strong> for the same run from memory, in <strong>41.3 s against 39.4 s</strong>. Both measure the same 915,488 points. The gap is smaller than the raw pair because the file-backed run also holds that pair in memory. A <code>UInt16</code> or <code>UInt8</code> scene, which is how Landsat level-1 imagery ships, holds a half or a quarter as much.</p><h2 id="Budget-for-both" tabindex="-1">Budget for both <a class="header-anchor" href="#Budget-for-both" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Budget for both {#Budget-for-both}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Peak is the blocks plus, when it is cached, the raw pair. Size the blocks against the halo on each axis, then decide whether the pair&#39;s own bytes are worth the runtime saved at small blocks.</p><p>The full measured record, including how to measure peak memory correctly and where the floor comes from, is in <a href="/AutoRIFT.jl/dev/explanation/memory#Memory">Memory</a>.</p>',39)])])}const k=t(o,[["render",r]]);export{g as __pageData,k as default};
